    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Gunnar Henderson** headlining the offense, boasting a team-high 81.88 efficiency score. With a favorable 5.20 xERA matchup against Fedde and a prime leadoff spot, **Gunnar Henderson** is our primary engine for Runs and HR production. **Kyle Tucker** (76.93) and **Andy Pages** (72.17) provide excellent secondary support; both benefit from a 10% park factor bump and a +11% SP skill advantage against Springs, making them elite starts despite the neutral platoon conditions. **Miguel Vargas** also draws a strong 66.77 projection, anchored by a high 0.370 xwOBA and a lucrative #2 batting order position, which provides significant upside in the RBI category.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the marginal bench tier, where **Curtis Mead** (39.15) narrowly missed the final cut. While **Curtis Mead** carries decent park-adjusted value, the heavy SP skill penalty against Early (-16%) pushed his efficiency below our 40.0 baseline floor, forcing a benching to preserve game caps. Conversely, we are keeping a close watch on **Nasim Nunez** (45.92); despite a weaker raw projection, his +4% score boost for facing a slow-delivery pitcher underscores our strategy of prioritizing stolen base opportunities in this 5x5 format. \n\nRisks today center largely on the **Jackson Merrill** and **Josh Lowe** slots. Both hitters carry sub-50 efficiency scores, weighed down by the difficult 3.46 xERA of the opposing starter, Woo, and negative park factors. While we are starting them to maintain depth, their lower scores suggest they are the most likely candidates for late-inning defensive substitutions if the game script turns negative. We remain cautious regarding **Nolan Schanuel**, whose 42.30 score is teetering on the edge of the floor; his matchup against a high-skill arm like Woo limits his ceiling, but his #3 spot in the order keeps him marginally viable for today\u2019s contest.",
